QB.add(23,[{"id":23001,"t":"The Human Genome","s":"Which definition of precision medicine is correct?","o":["Data-driven care accounting for genes, environment and lifestyle","Data-driven care accounting for genes alone, ignoring environment and lifestyle","Treatment chosen by guideline doses for the average patient","Surgery guided precisely by imaging","The study of heredity in families"],"e":"<b>Precision medicine</b>: data-driven treatment and prevention accounting for individual variability in <b>genes, environment and lifestyle</b>. Genetics used to be niche; cheaper, accessible genome analysis has brought it into widespread healthcare. <b>Genetics</b> = study of heredity; <b>gene</b> = unit of information encoding a protein or regulatory molecule.","w":["Environment and lifestyle too.","Individual variability.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Precision medicine definition.","rn":[["","Precision Medicine","Precision Medicine","Emerging data-driven approach; Disease treatment and prevention; Accounts for individual variability in genes, environment and lifestyle"]]},{"id":23002,"t":"The Human Genome","s":"A gene encodes a protein that is either structural or functional. Which is a functional protein example?","o":["An enzyme, receptor or signalling molecule","A phospholipid forming part of the cell membrane","A carbohydrate stored for energy","A nucleotide","A chromosome"],"e":"A <b>gene</b> is a unit of biological information encoding a specific <b>protein or regulatory molecule</b>; the protein is structural or functional – e.g. an <b>enzyme, receptor or signalling molecule</b>.","w":["Not protein.","Not protein.","Not protein.","Not protein."],"k":"Gene → structural or functional protein.","rn":[["","What 2 broad kinds of protein can a gene encode?","What 2 broad kinds of protein can a gene encode?","Structural (a cell component); Functional (e.g. enzyme, receptor, signalling molecule)"]]},{"id":23003,"t":"The Human Genome","f":"hg07","s":"Where is the genome located, and what is the central dogma?","o":["In the nucleus; DNA → RNA → protein","In the nucleus; RNA → DNA → protein","In the cytoplasm; DNA → RNA → protein","In ribosomes; protein → RNA → DNA","In mitochondria only; DNA → protein"],"e":"The genome is in the <b>nucleus</b>. Central dogma: DNA is <b>transcribed into RNA</b> and <b>translated into protein</b>. (Mitochondria also have their own small genome.)","w":["Order.","Nucleus.","Not.","Mostly nucleus."],"k":"Nucleus; DNA → RNA → protein.","rn":[["","What are the 2 steps of the central dogma?","What are the 2 steps of the central dogma?","Transcription (DNA to RNA); Translation (RNA to protein)"]]},{"id":23004,"t":"Structure of DNA","f":"hg09","s":"In a DNA nucleotide, to which carbon of the pentose is the base attached, and by what bond?","o":["Carbon 1′, by a glycosidic bond","Carbon 3′, by a phosphodiester bond","Carbon 5′, by a hydrogen bond","Carbon 2′, by an ionic bond","Carbon 4′, by a peptide bond"],"e":"DNA is a <b>polymer</b> of four nucleotides (A, T, C, G). Each base is attached to a 5′-phosphorylated pentose via a <b>glycosidic bond</b> on <b>carbon 1′</b>; forming it releases one water molecule. At 2′: OH = ribose (RNA), H = deoxyribose (DNA).","w":["3′ forms the phosphodiester link.","5′ carries phosphate.","2′ distinguishes ribose.","Not."],"k":"Base on C1′ via glycosidic bond.","rn":[["","Glycosidic Bond","Glycosidic Bond","Joins the nitrogenous base to the sugar; A bond involving a sugar (carbohydrate); Releases one water molecule when it forms"]]},{"id":23005,"t":"Structure of DNA","s":"Which bases are purines and which are pyrimidines?","o":["Purines: A and G (two rings); pyrimidines: C, U and T (one ring)","Purines: C, U and T (two rings); pyrimidines: A and G (one ring)","Purines: A and G (one ring); pyrimidines: C, U and T (two rings)","Purines: A and T (two rings); pyrimidines: G, C and U (one ring)","All five bases are purines with two rings"],"e":"<b>Purines</b>: adenine, guanine (two rings). <b>Pyrimidines</b>: cytosine, uracil, thymine (one ring). Nitrogenous bases contain nitrogen.","w":["Swapped.","Rings swapped.","T is pyrimidine.","Not."],"k":"Purines A, G; pyrimidines C, U, T.","rn":[["","Purines vs pyrimidines: number of rings","Purines vs pyrimidines: number of rings","Purines: 2 rings; Pyrimidines: 1 ring"]]},{"id":23006,"t":"Structure of DNA","s":"A free nucleotide arrives with three phosphates. What happens when it is added to the growing DNA chain?","o":["Two are lost as pyrophosphate; one is kept","One is lost as phosphate; two are kept","All three are kept in the backbone","All three are lost as pyrophosphate","They are converted to sugar"],"e":"A free nucleotide normally has <b>three phosphate</b> groups (like ATP). When added, <b>two are lost and one is kept</b> – the pyrophosphate released is split into phosphate ions.","w":["Two lost.","One kept.","One kept.","Not."],"k":"Lose two phosphates, keep one."},{"id":23007,"t":"Structure of DNA","s":"What distinguishes a nucleoside from a nucleotide?","o":["Nucleoside = base + sugar; nucleotide adds 1–3 phosphates","Nucleoside = base + sugar + phosphate; nucleotide lacks phosphate","Nucleoside = base only; nucleotide adds sugar","They are identical terms","Nucleoside is RNA only"],"e":"<b>Nucleoside</b> = base + sugar; with 1, 2 or 3 phosphate groups = nucleoside mono/di/triphosphate.","w":["Swapped.","Sugar.","Different.","Both."],"k":"Nucleoside = base + sugar.","rn":[["","Nucleoside","Nucleoside","Base + sugar; No phosphate"],["","What is a nucleoside with 3 phosphates called?","What is a nucleoside with 3 phosphates called?","Nucleoside triphosphate"]]},{"id":23008,"t":"Structure of DNA","f":"hg10","s":"Between which carbons does the phosphodiester bond form, and to which end are new nucleotides added?","o":["3′ and 5′ carbons; added to the 3′ end","3′ and 5′ carbons; added to the 5′ end","1′ and 2′ carbons; added to the 3′ end","2′ and 4′ carbons; added to either end","Between bases; added to the middle"],"e":"Sugars are connected via phosphate groups – the <b>phosphodiester bond</b> between the <b>3′ and 5′</b> carbons. <b>New nucleotides are added to the 3′ –OH end</b>; DNA grows 5′ → 3′. 5′ and 3′ ends are landmarks.","w":["3′.","3′–5′.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Phosphodiester 3′–5′; add at 3′.","rn":[["","What does \"3' end\" refer to?","What does \"3' end\" refer to?","The 3rd carbon of the pentose sugar; Where new nucleotides are added"]]},{"id":23009,"t":"Structure of DNA","s":"Why is the whole DNA molecule negatively charged?","o":["Each backbone phosphate carries a negative charge","Each base carries a negative charge","Each deoxyribose sugar carries a negative charge","Hydrogen bonds carry a negative charge","Histones add negative charge"],"e":"The circled minus sign on each phosphate is a <b>negative charge</b>, giving the whole DNA molecule a negative charge.","w":["Phosphate.","Phosphate.","Not.","Positive."],"k":"Phosphates make DNA negative.","rn":[["","What charge does the DNA molecule carry?","What charge does the DNA molecule carry?","Negative"],["","Where does the negative charge of DNA come from?","Where does the negative charge of DNA come from?","The phosphate groups"]]},{"id":23010,"t":"Structure of DNA","f":"hg11","s":"Which statement about the DNA double helix is correct?","o":["Antiparallel, 2 nm wide, 3.4 nm per turn, major and minor grooves","Parallel, 2 nm wide, 3.4 nm per turn, major and minor grooves","Antiparallel, 3.4 nm wide, 2 nm per turn, major and minor grooves","Antiparallel, variable width, no grooves","A single strand, 2 nm wide"],"e":"Double helix: two strands of <b>opposite polarity</b> (<b>antiparallel</b>), <b>uniform diameter</b> (2 nm), complementary bases, edges exposed in <b>major and minor grooves</b>; <b>3.4 nm</b> per turn; <b>0.34 nm</b> between bases.","w":["Antiparallel.","Swapped.","Uniform.","Double."],"k":"Helix dimensions.","rn":[["","What are the 4 key features of the double helix?","What are the 4 key features of the double helix?","Double stranded, uniform diameter; Antiparallel; Opposite bases are complementary; Base edges exposed in major and minor grooves"]]},{"id":23011,"t":"Structure of DNA","s":"Why do the major and minor grooves of DNA matter functionally?","o":["Proteins bind DNA within them","They store chemical energy","Histones are covalently bonded in them","Replication always starts in them","They have no function"],"e":"The grooves matter for <b>how proteins bind DNA</b>: some bind in the major groove, some in the minor.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not stated.","They do."],"k":"Grooves = protein binding sites.","rn":[["","Why do the major and minor grooves matter?","Why do the major and minor grooves matter?","Proteins bind DNA there"]]},{"id":23012,"t":"Structure of DNA","s":"Why do base pairs line up evenly in the helix, giving a uniform diameter?","o":["A two-ring base always pairs with a one-ring base","A two-ring purine always pairs with another two-ring purine","All four bases are exactly the same size","Histones space the bases evenly","Phosphates fill gaps between unequal pairs"],"e":"Base pairing A–T and G–C: a two-ring base always pairs with a one-ring base, so pairs have the <b>same length</b>.","w":["Purine–pyrimidine.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Purine–pyrimidine pairing → uniform width.","rn":[["","Why do all base pairs have the same length?","Why do all base pairs have the same length?","Each is one purine + one pyrimidine"]]},{"id":23013,"t":"Structure of DNA","s":"Whose X-ray crystallographic image revealed the DNA double helix?","e":"Rosalind Franklin's crystallography revealed the helix.","k":"Rosalind Franklin.","a":["rosalind franklin","franklin"],"rn":[["","Who obtained the first experimental data for the double helix?","Who obtained the first experimental data for the double helix?","Rosalind Franklin"]]},{"id":23014,"t":"Structure of DNA","f":"hg12","s":"How many hydrogen bonds join A–T and G–C pairs?","o":["A–T: 2; G–C: 3","A–T: 3; G–C: 2","A–T: 2; G–C: 2","A–T: 3; G–C: 3","A–T: 1; G–C: 4"],"e":"<b>A–T: two</b>; <b>G–C: three</b> hydrogen bonds. Memory aid: C and G curve like a 3; A and T straight lines, 2.","w":["Swapped.","G–C 3.","A–T 2.","Not."],"k":"A–T 2, G–C 3.","rn":[["","Image card: How many hydrogen bonds join this base pair?","How many hydrogen bonds join this base pair?","2 (A and T)"],["","Image card: How many hydrogen bonds join this base pair?","How many hydrogen bonds join this base pair?","3 (C and G)"]]},{"id":23015,"t":"Structure of DNA","s":"Why are hydrogen bonds well suited to holding the two DNA strands together?","o":["Stable, yet easily broken for replication","They are covalent and unbreakable, so the DNA is fully protected","Very strong, needing huge energy to break","They keep DNA single-stranded","They attach histones to DNA"],"e":"Hydrogen bonds satisfy two conditions: <b>stability</b> of the helix and the ability to <b>open up</b> for replication and transcription without much energy or effort.","w":["Not covalent.","Easily broken.","Double.","Not."],"k":"H-bonds: stable yet openable."},{"id":23016,"t":"Structure of DNA","s":"A DNA region rich in G–C pairs would be expected to:","o":["Be held more strongly, with three bonds per pair","Be held more weakly, with two bonds per pair","Be held more strongly, with two bonds per pair","Be single-stranded","Contain uracil"],"e":"G–C pairs have <b>three</b> hydrogen bonds vs two for A–T, so G–C-rich regions are more strongly held.","w":["Stronger.","Three.","Double.","DNA."],"k":"G–C rich = more H-bonds.","rn":[["","Image card: How many hydrogen bonds join this base pair?","How many hydrogen bonds join this base pair?","2 (A and T)"],["","Image card: How many hydrogen bonds join this base pair?","How many hydrogen bonds join this base pair?","3 (C and G)"]]},{"id":23017,"t":"DNA Replication","f":"hg14","s":"Why does the antiparallel structure of DNA create an “interesting issue” for replication?","o":["Nucleotides add only to 3′ ends, so new strands grow oppositely","Nucleotides add only to 5′ ends, so new strands grow the same way","Both new strands grow in the same direction","DNA cannot be replicated at all","Replication needs no enzymes"],"e":"Nucleotides must be added to the <b>3′ end</b>; with antiparallel strands, <b>one new strand grows one way and the other the opposite way</b>. DNA polymerase III adds the incoming nucleotide, releasing pyrophosphate.","w":["3′.","Opposite.","Not.","Not."],"k":"3′-only addition + antiparallel = issue.","rn":[["","What do the different domains of DNA polymerase do?","What do the different domains of DNA polymerase do?","Hold the DNA strands; Help add nucleotides"],["","Why does adding only to the 3' end cause a problem?","Why does adding only to the 3' end cause a problem?","The strands are antiparallel; So both strands cannot grow the same way"],["","What is released when a nucleotide is added to the growing strand?","What is released when a nucleotide is added to the growing strand?","Pyrophosphate ion; Then phosphate ions"]]},{"id":23018,"t":"DNA Replication","s":"Why were DNA replication processes first studied in bacteria?","o":["Replication is conserved across organisms","Replication differs completely between bacteria and humans","Humans lack DNA polymerase","Bacteria have nuclei that are easier to study","Bacteria are larger than human cells"],"e":"DNA polymerase was among the first proteins studied – first in bacteria, then mammals. These are <b>fundamental, conserved processes</b>, so they can be studied in smaller organisms.","w":["Conserved.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Replication is conserved.","rn":[["","Why can replication be studied in bacteria?","Why can replication be studied in bacteria?","Fundamental processes are conserved; Same in unicellular and multicellular organisms"]]},{"id":23019,"t":"DNA Replication","f":"hg15","s":"Which statement correctly contrasts the leading and lagging strands?","o":["Leading: continuous with the fork; lagging: discontinuous Okazaki fragments","Leading: discontinuous Okazaki fragments; lagging: continuous with the fork","Both strands are synthesised continuously","Both strands are synthesised in fragments","Leading needs multiple primers; lagging needs one"],"e":"Due to antiparallel DNA, replication needs a <b>leading strand</b> (follows the fork, <b>continuous</b>) and a <b>lagging strand</b> (<b>discontinuous</b>, Okazaki fragments). Overall: <b>semi-conservative</b>.","w":["Swapped.","Not.","Not.","Reversed."],"k":"Leading continuous; lagging fragments.","rn":[["","Leading vs lagging strand: synthesis","Leading vs lagging strand: synthesis","Leading: continuous; Lagging: discontinuous (fragments)"]]},{"id":23020,"t":"DNA Replication","s":"Why is the lagging strand made in small fragments rather than continuously?","o":["Opposing polymerases would collide and fall off","The lagging strand has no template to copy continuously from","Fragments are faster to make than continuous strands","Ligase only works on fragments","Primers cannot bind continuously"],"e":"If both worked continuously, they'd travel opposite ways and <b>bump into each other</b>; since polymerase <b>falls off at a physical stop</b> (it is processive), both could fall off → <b>incomplete replication</b>. So the lagging strand is made “little bit and falls off”.","w":["It has a template.","Not stated.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Why Okazaki fragments.","rn":[["","Why is the lagging strand made in small fragments?","Why is the lagging strand made in small fragments?","Otherwise the 2 polymerases would bump; Not enough space; Both would fall off"]]},{"id":23021,"t":"DNA Replication","s":"Each daughter DNA molecule contains one old and one new strand. Name this mode of replication.","e":"DNA replication is semi-conservative.","k":"Semi-conservative.","a":["semi-conservative","semiconservative","semi conservative"],"rn":[["","Semi-conservative Replication","Semi-conservative Replication","Each new DNA molecule has one original strand and one new strand"]]},{"id":23022,"t":"DNA Replication","s":"Which proteins help open the DNA at the replication fork, and why are other proteins needed to keep it open?","o":["Helicase and topoisomerase; hydrogen bonds re-form spontaneously","Ligase and primase; hydrogen bonds re-form spontaneously","Helicase and topoisomerase; histones re-bind spontaneously","DNA polymerase alone; it keeps the fork open","Exonuclease; primers keep it open"],"e":"Opening requires breaking hydrogen bonds, helped by <b>helicase</b> and <b>topoisomerase</b> (“a bit additional”). Other proteins keep DNA open because <b>hydrogen bonds can re-form spontaneously</b>.","w":["Not.","H-bonds.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Helicase/topoisomerase open DNA.","rn":[["","Why are extra proteins bound to opened single strands?","Why are extra proteins bound to opened single strands?","To keep it open; Hydrogen bonds re-form spontaneously"],["","Do hydrogen bonds re-form spontaneously?","Do hydrogen bonds re-form spontaneously?","Yes; So opened DNA can close again"],["","What 2 proteins help open the DNA?","What 2 proteins help open the DNA?","Helicase; Topoisomerase"]]},{"id":23023,"t":"DNA Replication","s":"Why does DNA polymerase need an RNA primer?","o":["It cannot start de novo; it needs a free 3′ end","It cannot start de novo; it needs a free 5′ end","Primers supply energy for polymerisation","Primers unwind the DNA","Primers join Okazaki fragments"],"e":"Polymerase adds nucleotides to a 3′ end but <b>cannot add the starting nucleotide</b> (not de novo) – chemically impossible. A short <b>RNA primer</b> gives a free 3′ end. Leading strand: <b>one primer</b>; lagging: <b>multiple primers</b>.","w":["3′.","Not.","Helicase.","Ligase."],"k":"Primer gives free 3′ end.","rn":[["","Why does DNA polymerase need a primer?","Why does DNA polymerase need a primer?","It cannot add nucleotides de novo; Needs a free 3' end"]]},{"id":23024,"t":"DNA Replication","s":"How many primers do the leading and lagging strands require?","o":["Leading: one; lagging: multiple","Leading: multiple; lagging: one","Leading: one; lagging: one","Leading: two; lagging: two","Neither needs primers"],"e":"Leading strand has <b>one primer</b>; lagging strand has <b>multiple primers</b> (one per Okazaki fragment).","w":["Swapped.","Multiple.","Not.","Needed."],"k":"1 vs many primers.","rn":[["","Leading vs lagging strand: number of primers","Leading vs lagging strand: number of primers","Leading: one primer; Lagging: multiple primers"]]},{"id":23025,"t":"DNA Replication","s":"After replication, what happens to RNA primers and Okazaki fragments?","o":["Primers become DNA; ligase joins fragments","Primers stay as RNA; ligase joins fragments","Primers become DNA; helicase joins fragments","Primers stay as RNA; fragments are degraded","Histones replace the primers"],"e":"When replication has finished, the <b>RNA primer is replaced by DNA</b> and Okazaki fragments are <b>joined by ligase</b>. Okazaki fragments are named after the person who first described them.","w":["Replaced.","Ligase.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Primers replaced; ligase joins.","rn":[["","What joins the Okazaki fragments together?","What joins the Okazaki fragments together?","Ligase"]]},{"id":23026,"t":"DNA Replication","s":"Why does every chromosome have multiple replication forks?","o":["DNA is so large it can’t run end to end","Only one fork per chromosome is possible","Forks occur only at telomeres","Mitochondria require multiple forks","To introduce useful mutations"],"e":"Because our DNA is large, <b>every chromosome has multiple replication forks</b>, all over the DNA in a single nucleus.","w":["Multiple.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Multiple forks per chromosome.","rn":[["","Why does each chromosome have many replication forks?","Why does each chromosome have many replication forks?","Our DNA is very large; Does not copy end to end"]]},{"id":23027,"t":"DNA Replication","f":"hg24","s":"In the step-by-step cartoon, why is synthesis on one strand discontinuous?","o":["It grows away from the fork, needing new primers","It grows towards the fork, needing no primers","It has no template to copy","It is made of RNA","It is degraded and rebuilt"],"e":"Step 7: synthesis on the other strand <b>requires further primer binding</b> and is <b>discontinuous (lagging)</b>; the leading strand is continuous (step 6).","w":["Leading.","It has.","DNA.","Not."],"k":"Lagging needs repeated primers.","rn":[["","What are the steps of replication in order?","What are the steps of replication in order?","DNA unwinds and opens; RNA primers bind; 5' to 3' synthesis starts on both strands; DNA keeps unwinding; Leading strand continuous; Lagging strand needs more primers"],["","Lagging Strand","Lagging Strand","Synthesised discontinuously; In small fragments (Okazaki fragments); Needs further primer binding"]]},{"id":23028,"t":"DNA Replication","s":"DNA polymerase adds T opposite G. Name the activity that removes and replaces it.","e":"Exonuclease proofreading removes the end nucleotide; endonucleases cut within a strand.","k":"Exonuclease proofreading.","a":["exonuclease","exonuclease proofreading","proofreading","3-5 exonuclease","3' to 5' exonuclease"],"rn":[["","Exonuclease Activity","Exonuclease Activity","Removing the final nucleotide of a chain"],["","Why is exonuclease activity important for DNA polymerase?","Why is exonuclease activity important for DNA polymerase?","Proofreading; Corrects mistakes"]]},{"id":23029,"t":"DNA Replication","s":"Which statement distinguishes exonuclease from endonuclease activity?","o":["Exonuclease removes an end nucleotide; endonuclease cuts within","Exonuclease cuts within; endonuclease removes an end nucleotide","Both add nucleotides to the chain","Both join fragments","They are identical"],"e":"<b>Exonuclease</b> = removing a final nucleotide; <b>endonuclease</b> = cutting through a DNA chain. Memory aid: exo = exit from the end; endo = cut inside.","w":["Swapped.","Remove.","Ligase.","Different."],"k":"Exo = end; endo = inside.","rn":[["","Exonuclease Activity","Exonuclease Activity","Removing the final nucleotide of a chain"]]},{"id":23030,"t":"DNA Replication","s":"Why is understanding DNA replication important for clinical laboratories?","o":["It enabled PCR for diagnosis and treatment","It enabled electron microscopy of chromosomes in pathology","It created vaccines only","It is used only in bacteria","It has no clinical use"],"e":"Describing replication allowed development of <b>PCR</b>, routine in every lab, including <b>pathology and analytical labs</b> analysing genetic material to <b>determine treatment, disease and prognosis</b>.","w":["PCR.","Not.","Not.","It has."],"k":"Replication → PCR.","rn":[["","What 3 clinical uses do PCR-based assays have?","What 3 clinical uses do PCR-based assays have?","Determine treatment; Determine disease; Look at prognosis"]]},{"id":23031,"t":"DNA Packaging and Organisation","s":"Which statement about euchromatin and heterochromatin is correct?","o":["Euchromatin ~1000-fold, needed genes; heterochromatin ~10,000-fold, unneeded","Euchromatin ~10,000-fold, unneeded genes; heterochromatin ~1000-fold, needed","Both ~1000-fold, with needed genes","Both uncompacted","Euchromatin is in mitochondria"],"e":"<b>Euchromatin</b>: c.<b>1000-fold</b>, loosely packed, genes the cell <b>needs</b> (accessible, transcribed). <b>Heterochromatin</b> and metaphase chromosomes: c.<b>10,000-fold</b>, tightly packed, genes <b>not needed</b>. Analogy: heterochromatin = putting something in the loft.","w":["Swapped.","Different.","Compacted.","Nucleus."],"k":"Eu 1000×; hetero 10,000×.","rn":[["","Heterochromatin","Heterochromatin","Tightly packed chromatin; Compacted c.10,000 fold; Genes the cell does not need"],["","Euchromatin","Euchromatin","Loosely packed chromatin; Compacted c.1000 fold; Genes the cell needs"]]},{"id":23032,"t":"DNA Packaging and Organisation","s":"All cells contain the same DNA, yet a bone cell makes collagen but never neurotransmitters. How does chromatin packaging help explain this?","o":["Needed genes loose; unneeded genes packed away","Bone cells simply lack the genes needed for neurotransmitters","Histones are absent from bone cells","Mutations remove unneeded genes","Bone cells have no nucleus"],"e":"Packing saves space and allows <b>selective gene expression</b>: all cells have the same DNA but use different proteins; genes needed are accessible (euchromatin), others packed away.","w":["Same DNA.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Packaging → selective expression.","rn":[["","Euchromatin","Euchromatin","Loosely packed chromatin; Compacted c.1000 fold; Genes the cell needs"],["","Heterochromatin","Heterochromatin","Tightly packed chromatin; Compacted c.10,000 fold; Genes the cell does not need"]]},{"id":23033,"t":"DNA Packaging and Organisation","f":"hg28","s":"What is the composition of a nucleosome?","o":["8 histones (2 each of H2A, H2B, H3, H4) with 146 bp of DNA","8 histones (2 each of H2A, H2B, H3, H4) with 1000 bp of DNA","4 histones (1 each of H2A, H2B, H3, H4) with 146 bp of DNA","8 copies of H1 with 146 bp of DNA","One histone with RNA"],"e":"The fundamental unit is the <b>nucleosome</b>: <b>8 histones</b> (2 each of H2A, H2B, H3, H4) wrapped with <b>146 bp</b> of DNA. Nucleosomes form “beads on a string”, further coiled into fibres and loops. (H = histone.)","w":["146.","Two each.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Nucleosome composition.","rn":[["","Image card: What is this structure? What is its core made of?","What is this structure? What is its core made of?","Nucleosome; Octamer: 2 copies H2A, H2B, H3, H4; 146 bp DNA wrapped around"],["","What are the 4 histones of the octameric core?","What are the 4 histones of the octameric core?","H2A; H2B; H3; H4"],["","Nucleosome","Nucleosome","8 histones (octamer); Wrapped with 146 bp of DNA"]]},{"id":23034,"t":"DNA Packaging and Organisation","f":"hg29","s":"Which sequence describes DNA packaging from least to most compact?","o":["Helix → beads on a string → fibre → loops → tighter coils → chromosome","Helix → fibre → beads on a string → loops → tighter coils → chromosome","Beads on a string → helix → fibre → loops → chromosome","Helix → loops → beads on a string → fibre → chromosome","Chromosome → fibre → beads → helix"],"e":"DNA wraps around protein cores (beads on a string), then coils into fibres, folds into loops, coils more tightly, and forms the chromosome.","w":["Beads first.","Helix first.","Fibre before loops.","Reversed."],"k":"Packaging levels.","rn":[["","What are the levels of DNA packaging in order?","What are the levels of DNA packaging in order?","Double helix; Beads on a string (nucleosomes); Fibres; Loops; Chromosome"],["","Image card: What levels of DNA packaging are shown, left to right?","What levels of DNA packaging are shown, left to right?","Double helix; Beads on a string; Fibres; Loops; Chromosome"],["","What happens to the beads on a string next?","What happens to the beads on a string next?","Further coiled into fibres and loops"]]},{"id":23035,"t":"DNA Packaging and Organisation","s":"When does DNA condense into visible chromosomes?","o":["During division, so chromosomes can separate","During S phase, so that the DNA can be copied more easily","At all times in every cell","Only after cell death","Only in mitochondria"],"e":"Outside cell division DNA is less packed; for <b>cell division</b> it packs more into chromosomes so they <b>can be separated</b>. Chromosomes are only visible at certain stages.","w":["Division.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Chromosomes form for division.","rn":[["","What form is DNA in outside cell division?","What form is DNA in outside cell division?","Looser packing; Not visible chromosomes"],["","Why does DNA pack into chromosomes during division?","Why does DNA pack into chromosomes during division?","So it can be separated"],["","When are chromosomes visible?","When are chromosomes visible?","Only at certain stages of the cell cycle; Cell division"]]},{"id":23036,"t":"DNA Packaging and Organisation","s":"Name the display of a person's chromosomes arranged in numbered pairs (1–22 + X/Y).","e":"A karyotype shows chromosomes in pairs.","k":"Karyotype.","a":["karyotype","karyogram"],"rn":[["","Image card: What is this called? What does it contain?","What is this called? What does it contain?","Karyotype; Chromosomes 1 to 22; Sex chromosomes X/Y"],["","What chromosomes make up the human karyotype?","What chromosomes make up the human karyotype?","Chromosomes 1 to 22; Plus X/Y"],["","Karyotype","Karyotype","A chromosome spread"]]},{"id":23037,"t":"The Cell Cycle and Mitosis","f":"hg33","s":"Which phases make up interphase, and what happens in S phase?","o":["G1, S and G2; S = DNA synthesis","M and cytokinesis; S = splitting","G0 and M; S = sugar synthesis","Prophase and metaphase; S = spindle","G1 only; S = senescence"],"e":"<b>Interphase = G1, S, G2</b>. G1: resting (Gap 1); non-dividing cells arrest in <b>G0</b>. <b>S</b>: DNA <b>synthesis</b>. G2: Gap 2. Then mitosis and cytokinesis.","w":["M phase.","Not.","Mitosis.","Not."],"k":"Interphase = G1, S, G2.","rn":[["","S stage","S stage","DNA synthesis"],["","What happens in the S stage?","What happens in the S stage?","DNA synthesis (replication)"]]},{"id":23038,"t":"The Cell Cycle and Mitosis","s":"A neuron will never divide again. Name the cell-cycle state it occupies.","e":"G0: cells that have left the cycle; most cells are in G1.","k":"G0.","a":["g0","g zero","g0 phase","g-0"],"rn":[["","G1 vs G0","G1 vs G0","G1: resting, can still divide; G0: non-dividing, never divides again"]]},{"id":23039,"t":"The Cell Cycle and Mitosis","s":"What does a cell do in G2?","o":["Makes proteins and organelles for division","Replicates its DNA ready for division into two cells","Separates its chromosomes","Arrests permanently","Re-forms its nuclear envelope"],"e":"G2 is shorter and leads to mitosis: the cell <b>prepares</b> by making more <b>proteins and organelles</b> – two cells need “more of everything”.","w":["S phase.","M.","G0.","Telophase."],"k":"G2 = preparation.","rn":[["","What happens in G2?","What happens in G2?","Cell prepares for division; Makes more proteins and organelles"]]},{"id":23040,"t":"The Cell Cycle and Mitosis","f":"hg35","s":"At the G1 checkpoint, what is checked?","o":["DNA damage and a favourable environment","Spindle attachment of chromosomes","Completion of DNA replication","Completion of cytokinesis","Histone number"],"e":"<b>G1</b>: stops the cycle if <b>DNA damaged</b> or <b>unfavourable environment</b> (nutrients; signal still present). This matters because a cell that replicates its DNA must divide.","w":["M.","G2.","Not.","Not."],"k":"G1 checkpoint checks.","rn":[["","What 2 things are checked at G1?","What 2 things are checked at G1?","DNA damage; Unfavourable environment"]]},{"id":23041,"t":"The Cell Cycle and Mitosis","s":"A cell has replicated its DNA, but the G2 checkpoint detects damage it cannot repair. What happens?","o":["Repair or die; it can’t stay with double DNA","It returns safely to G1 and waits there with double DNA","It divides anyway, passing on the damage","It enters G0 permanently with double DNA","It becomes a stem cell"],"e":"At the end of S/G2, DNA damage or incomplete replication → the cell tries to repair; if it cannot, it <b>dies</b>, because it can't exist with double DNA (would make double proteins).","w":["Cannot.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"After S: repair or die.","rn":[["","Why must a cell die if it cannot repair after S phase?","Why must a cell die if it cannot repair after S phase?","It has double the DNA; Would make double the proteins"]]},{"id":23042,"t":"The Cell Cycle and Mitosis","s":"If the G1 checkpoint finds too few nutrients, what happens to the cell?","o":["It stays in G1 without dividing","It must die by apoptosis","It replicates its DNA anyway","It enters mitosis anyway","It becomes cancerous"],"e":"Student Q: not necessarily death – the cell <b>simply doesn't divide</b> and stays in G1.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"G1 failure: stay in G1.","rn":[["","Can a cell stay with replicated DNA without dividing?","Can a cell stay with replicated DNA without dividing?","No"]]},{"id":23043,"t":"The Cell Cycle and Mitosis","s":"What controls cell cycle checkpoints?","o":["Cyclins and protein kinases","Histones and protein kinases","Cyclins and DNA ligase","Primers and ribosomes","Helicases"],"e":"Checkpoints are <b>controlled by cyclins and protein kinases</b>.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Cyclins + kinases.","rn":[["","Checkpoints are controlled by what?","Checkpoints are controlled by what?","Cyclins; Protein kinases"]]},{"id":23044,"t":"The Cell Cycle and Mitosis","s":"The M checkpoint stops mitosis if:","o":["Chromosomes aren’t attached to the spindle","Nutrients are low, so the cell is too small to divide","DNA replication is incomplete","DNA is damaged before S phase","Histones are missing"],"e":"<b>M checkpoint</b>: chromosomes not attached to the spindle → process aborted (cell dies).","w":["This checkpoint checks spindle attachment, not nutrients.","G2.","G1.","Not."],"k":"M checkpoint: spindle attachment.","rn":[["","What is checked in mitosis?","What is checked in mitosis?","Chromosomes attached to the mitotic spindle"]]},{"id":23045,"t":"The Cell Cycle and Mitosis","s":"A patient on chemotherapy develops anaemia and hair loss. What explains this?","o":["It hits all fast-dividing cells, not only cancer","It hits S and M phases in cancer cells only, sparing all normal cells","It causes iron and protein deficiency","It affects only cells in G0","It blocks histones in hair"],"e":"Chemotherapy drugs damage DNA or interfere with replication/mitosis; checkpoints are not satisfied → cells stop dividing or die (apoptosis). But they affect <b>all dividing cells</b> – <b>bone marrow</b> (anaemia), <b>hair</b> – “a very crude way” to treat malignancy.","w":["All dividing.","Not.","Dividing.","Not."],"k":"Chemo hits all dividing cells.","rn":[["","Do chemotherapy drugs only affect cancer cells?","Do chemotherapy drugs only affect cancer cells?","No; All dividing cells"],["","Why do chemotherapy drugs target malignant cells?","Why do chemotherapy drugs target malignant cells?","They are fast dividing"],["","What is cancer, in cell cycle terms?","What is cancer, in cell cycle terms?","Cells keep dividing without a need"]]},{"id":23046,"t":"The Cell Cycle and Mitosis","s":"How does cancer relate to cell division?","o":["Cells keep dividing without a need","Cells stop dividing when needed","Cells divide only after injury","Cells stay in G0","Cells divide normally"],"e":"Cancer: cells <b>keep dividing without a need</b>. Normal division occurs for growth or replacement (damaged epithelium, skin cuts).","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Cancer = unneeded division.","rn":[["","What is cancer, in cell cycle terms?","What is cancer, in cell cycle terms?","Cells keep dividing without a need"]]},{"id":23047,"t":"The Cell Cycle and Mitosis","s":"What is the outcome of mitosis?","o":["Two identical diploid nuclei from one parent","Two identical haploid nuclei from one parent","Four haploid nuclei from one parent","Two different nuclei from one parent","One nucleus with double DNA"],"e":"Mitosis: one parent nucleus → <b>two daughter nuclei</b>, genetically <b>identical and diploid</b>. Stages: prophase, metaphase, anaphase, telophase.","w":["Diploid.","Not.","Identical.","Separated."],"k":"Mitosis → 2 identical diploid nuclei.","rn":[["","How do the daughter nuclei compare to the parent?","How do the daughter nuclei compare to the parent?","Genetically identical; Diploid"]]},{"id":23048,"t":"The Cell Cycle and Mitosis","s":"The nuclear envelope breaks down and the mitotic spindle forms. Name the stage.","e":"P-M-A-T: prophase, metaphase, anaphase, telophase.","k":"Prophase.","a":["prophase"],"rn":[["","What 3 things happen in prophase?","What 3 things happen in prophase?","DNA packaged into chromosomes; Nuclear envelope dissolves; Mitotic spindle forms"],["","What are the 4 events of mitosis in order?","What are the 4 events of mitosis in order?","Prophase; Metaphase; Anaphase; Telophase; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":23049,"t":"The Cell Cycle and Mitosis","s":"Chromosomes align at the centre of the cell, attached via kinetochores to spindle microtubules. Name the stage.","e":"Metaphase: alignment at the equator; the M checkpoint checks spindle attachment.","k":"Metaphase.","a":["metaphase"],"rn":[["","What happens in metaphase?","What happens in metaphase?","Chromosomes align at the centre (metaphase plate)"]]},{"id":23050,"t":"The Cell Cycle and Mitosis","f":"hg39","s":"Kinetochore microtubules shorten, pulling chromosomes to opposite poles. Which stage?","o":["Anaphase","Metaphase","Prophase","Telophase","Cytokinesis"],"e":"<b>Anaphase</b>: the spindle separates each chromosome towards the two ends; spindle poles move apart.","w":["Alignment.","Formation.","Re-forming.","Cell split."],"k":"Anaphase separation.","rn":[["","In anaphase, what happens to the spindle poles?","In anaphase, what happens to the spindle poles?","Increasing separation"],["","In anaphase, what happens to kinetochore microtubules?","In anaphase, what happens to kinetochore microtubules?","They shorten"],["","What happens in anaphase?","What happens in anaphase?","Mitotic spindle separates chromosomes to the two ends"]]},{"id":23051,"t":"The Cell Cycle and Mitosis","f":"hg40","s":"The nuclear envelope re-forms and chromosomes decondense at each pole. Which stage?","o":["Telophase","Anaphase","Metaphase","Prophase","S phase"],"e":"<b>Telophase</b>: complete separation; nuclear envelope re-forms; chromosomes decondense. Memory aid: P-M-A-T.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Telophase.","rn":[["","In telophase, what happens to the nuclear envelope?","In telophase, what happens to the nuclear envelope?","Re-forms"],["","In telophase, what happens to the chromosomes?","In telophase, what happens to the chromosomes?","They decondense"]]},{"id":23052,"t":"The Human Genome","s":"Which comparison of the mitochondrial and nuclear genomes is correct?","o":["Mitochondrial: 16.6 kbp, 37 genes, maternal; nuclear: 3.2 × 10⁹ bp, ~20,000 genes","Mitochondrial: 3.2 × 10⁹ bp, 20,000 genes; nuclear: 16.6 kbp, 37 genes","Mitochondrial: 16.6 kbp, 37 genes, paternal; nuclear: 3.2 × 10⁹ bp, ~20,000 genes","Both: 3.2 × 10⁹ bp, ~20,000 genes","Mitochondrial: 16.6 kbp, 20,000 genes, maternal"],"e":"Mitochondrial: <b>16.6 kbp</b>, no repetitive sequence (87 bp intergenic in the D-loop), <b>22 tRNA, 2 rRNA, 13 protein-coding</b> genes, <b>maternal</b> inheritance. Nuclear: <b>3.2 billion bp</b>, majority high copy repetitive elements, <b>~20,000 genes → ~100,000 proteins</b>.","w":["Swapped.","Maternal.","Different.","37."],"k":"Mito vs nuclear genome.","rn":[["","How many genes in the nuclear genome?","How many genes in the nuclear genome?","~20,000; Range 20-25,000"]]},{"id":23053,"t":"The Human Genome","s":"A disease is caused by a variant in the mitochondrial genome. From which parent is it inherited?","e":"The mitochondrial genome (16.6 kbp, 37 genes) is maternally inherited.","k":"Mother.","a":["mother","maternal","the mother","maternally","mum"]},{"id":23055,"t":"The Human Genome","f":"hg47","s":"How can ~20,000 human genes produce ~100,000 proteins?","o":["Splicing combines exons in different ways into isoforms","Each gene is copied five times during replication","Introns code for extra proteins","Mutations create new proteins","Mitochondria make the extra proteins"],"e":"Genes have <b>exons</b> (coding) and <b>introns</b> (non-coding); DNA between genes = <b>intergenic</b>. <b>Splicing</b> combines exons differently – e.g. two exons used in only one isoform of TXLNG – so the <b>proteome is larger</b> than the genome (~5×).","w":["Not.","Non-coding.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Splicing → larger proteome.","rn":[["","Splicing","Splicing","One gene gives more than one protein; By combining exons already in the DNA sequence"],["","Isoform","Isoform","One protein version of a gene; Some exons used only in one isoform"]]},{"id":23056,"t":"The Human Genome","s":"Which statement about exons and introns is correct?","o":["Exons coding; introns non-coding","Introns coding; exons non-coding","Both coding","Both non-coding","Exons lie between genes"],"e":"<b>Exons are coding</b> (transcribed into RNA as used); <b>introns are non-coding</b>. (The lecturer initially misspoke and corrected it.)","w":["Swapped.","Introns not.","Exons code.","Intergenic."],"k":"Exons code; introns don't.","rn":[["","Introns","Introns","Non-coding parts of a gene"]]},{"id":23057,"t":"The Human Genome","s":"Why can genes on the same stretch of DNA run in opposite directions?","o":["The ATG start can be on either side","DNA has no direction, so genes are arranged at random","Introns reverse the direction of genes","Histones flip genes over","Genes are read only in one direction"],"e":"Genes can run in opposite directions, referring to the start of transcription (the <b>ATG</b>), which can be on either side.","w":["It has polarity.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Genes read in both directions."},{"id":23058,"t":"The Human Genome","s":"How common are SNPs in the human genome?","o":["About one per 1,330 bp; over 100 million known","About one per million bp; about 100 known","About one per chromosome; a few thousand known","About one per 1,330 bp; about 100 known","None are known"],"e":"On average one <b>SNP every 1,330 bp</b>; <b>>100 million</b> identified (most common variant). Larger structural variations also exist.","w":["Not.","Not.","100 million.","Many."],"k":"SNP every ~1,330 bp.","rn":[["","How many SNPs have been identified?","How many SNPs have been identified?","Over 100 million"]]},{"id":23059,"t":"The Human Genome","s":"Are SNPs mutations?","o":["No – they are normal differences between people","Yes – every SNP causes disease","No – they are large chromosomal deletions","Yes – they occur only in cancer","No – they are histone changes"],"e":"SNPs arise as errors that propagate but are <b>not mutations</b>; they differentiate every person's genome – e.g. <b>forensics</b> matches DNA to a person via unique SNP patterns.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"SNPs ≠ mutations.","rn":[["","Are SNPs mutations?","Are SNPs mutations?","No; Differences between people's genomes"]]},{"id":23060,"t":"The Human Genome","f":"hg48","s":"What determines phenotype?","o":["Genotype via development, plus environment","Genotype alone, regardless of development or environment","Environment alone, regardless of genotype","Random chance only","Histones only"],"e":"<b>Genotype → Development → Phenotype</b>, with <b>Environment</b> feeding into development.","w":["Not alone.","Genotype too.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Genes + environment → phenotype.","rn":[["","What is the order from genes to traits?","What is the order from genes to traits?","Genotype; Development (+ environment); Phenotype"]]},{"id":23061,"t":"The Human Genome","f":"hg49","s":"How does allele frequency relate to effect size in genetic variation and disease?","o":["Rare alleles: large effects; common variants: small effects","Rare alleles: small effects; common variants: large effects","All variants have equal effects","Frequency and effect are unrelated","Common alleles always cause severe disease"],"e":"Main band: <b>rare alleles causing Mendelian disease</b> (high effect, low frequency) → <b>common variants identified by GWAS</b> (low effect, high frequency). Outliers: rare variants of small effect; common variants with large effect. Very common variants rarely lead to anything sinister.","w":["Swapped.","Not.","Related.","Not."],"k":"Rare = big effect; common = small.","rn":[["","What are the 4 variant groups by frequency and effect?","What are the 4 variant groups by frequency and effect?","Rare alleles causing Mendelian disease (high effect); Rare variants of small effect; Common variants identified by GWAS (low effect); Common variants with a large effect"]]},{"id":23062,"t":"The Human Genome","s":"Name the type of study that typically identifies common, low-effect genetic variants.","e":"GWAS find common variants with small effects; rare alleles tend to have large effects.","k":"GWAS.","a":["gwas","genome-wide association study","genome wide association study","genome-wide association studies"],"rn":[["","What are the 4 variant groups by frequency and effect?","What are the 4 variant groups by frequency and effect?","Rare alleles causing Mendelian disease (high effect); Rare variants of small effect; Common variants identified by GWAS (low effect); Common variants with a large effect"]]},{"id":23063,"t":"The Human Genome","s":"Approximately how many phenotypes have a known molecular basis, according to OMIM?","o":["About 7000","About 70","About 700,000","About 20,000","None"],"e":"Approximately <b>7000 phenotypes</b> with known molecular basis (OMIM) – not a number to memorise; gives an idea of what we know.","w":["Not.","Not.","Gene number.","Many."],"k":"~7000 known.","rn":[["","For how many phenotypes is the molecular basis of disease known?","For how many phenotypes is the molecular basis of disease known?","About 7000"]]},{"id":23064,"t":"The Human Genome","s":"A child has intellectual disability, facial abnormalities and broad thumbs and great toes, with a CREBBP mutation. Name the syndrome.","e":"Rubinstein-Taybi syndrome (CREBBP, a histone acetyltransferase); Coffin-Lowry (RPS6KA3, X-linked) is related.","k":"Rubinstein-Taybi syndrome.","a":["rubinstein-taybi","rubinstein taybi","rubinstein-taybi syndrome","rubinstein taybi syndrome","rts"],"rn":[["","Rubinstein-Taybi vs Coffin-Lowry: gene","Rubinstein-Taybi vs Coffin-Lowry: gene","Rubinstein-Taybi: CREBBP; Coffin-Lowry: RPS6KA3"],["","What are the symptoms of Rubinstein-Taybi syndrome?","What are the symptoms of Rubinstein-Taybi syndrome?","Mental retardation; Facial abnormalities; Broad thumbs; Broad great toes"],["","Rubinstein-Taybi syndrome results from a mutation in what?","Rubinstein-Taybi syndrome results from a mutation in what?","CREBBP; A histone acetyl-transferase"]]},{"id":23065,"t":"The Human Genome","s":"Coffin-Lowry syndrome is caused by mutation in which gene, and how does it relate to CREBBP?","o":["RPS6KA3, which regulates CREBBP; X-linked","CREBBP itself; autosomal","RPS6KA3, which regulates CREBBP; autosomal","HER2, which regulates histones","BRCA1, which repairs DNA"],"e":"<b>Coffin-Lowry</b> (1 in 50–100,000): <b>X-linked</b> mental retardation, skeletal malformation, growth retardation, cognitive impairment; mutation in <b>RPS6KA3</b>, regulating <b>CREBBP</b>. Both involve proteins in histone function/DNA packaging.","w":["Not.","X-linked.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Coffin-Lowry = RPS6KA3.","rn":[["","What does RPS6KA3 do?","What does RPS6KA3 do?","Regulates CREBBP activity"]]},{"id":23066,"t":"The Human Genome","s":"What links Rubinstein-Taybi and Coffin-Lowry syndromes mechanistically?","o":["Both involve histone function","Both involve DNA polymerase proofreading errors","Both are mitochondrial diseases","Both are caused by SNPs","Both are chromosome deletions"],"e":"CREBBP is a <b>histone</b> acetyl-transferase and RPS6KA3 regulates it – both diseases come from faults in DNA packaging (section 4).","w":["Histones.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Packaging-related syndromes.","rn":[["","What process do both syndromes affect?","What process do both syndromes affect?","Histone function; DNA packaging"]]},{"id":23067,"t":"The Human Genome","s":"Why don't very significant variations that stop fundamental processes propagate through generations?","o":["They cause severe disease or death","They are too small to be passed on to offspring","They are always repaired before inheritance","They occur only in mitochondria","They are SNPs"],"e":"The more significant the protein affected, the more profound the outcome – severe disease or lethality; such variations <b>don't propagate</b>, while those with some effect can be inherited.","w":["Severe.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Lethal variants don't propagate.","rn":[["","Rare variants have what effect?","Rare variants have what effect?","High effect; Can cause disease"]]},{"id":23068,"t":"DNA Replication","s":"Which enzyme function is the basis of DNA polymerase's low error rate?","o":["Exonuclease proofreading","Endonuclease cutting of DNA","Ligase joining","Helicase unwinding","Primase priming"],"e":"DNA polymerase proofreads via exonuclease activity; otherwise “we would be full of mutations”.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Proofreading via exonuclease.","rn":[["","Why is exonuclease activity important for DNA polymerase?","Why is exonuclease activity important for DNA polymerase?","Proofreading; Corrects mistakes"]]},{"id":23069,"t":"DNA Replication","s":"Name the enzyme that joins Okazaki fragments on the lagging strand.","e":"After RNA primers are replaced with DNA, DNA ligase joins the fragments.","k":"DNA ligase.","a":["dna ligase","ligase"],"rn":[["","What joins the Okazaki fragments together?","What joins the Okazaki fragments together?","Ligase"]]},{"id":23070,"t":"DNA Replication","s":"Which statement about DNA polymerase being “processive” is correct?","o":["It adds until a physical stop, then falls off","It adds one nucleotide and then stops each time","It adds nucleotides to the 5′ end","It never falls off the template","It needs no template"],"e":"DNA polymerase is <b>processive</b> (heard as “progressive”): carries on until a physical stop, then falls off.","w":["Processive.","3′.","It does.","It does."],"k":"Processive polymerase.","rn":[["","When does DNA polymerase stop adding nucleotides?","When does DNA polymerase stop adding nucleotides?","When it hits a stop it cannot pass; Then it falls off"]]},{"id":23071,"t":"The Cell Cycle and Mitosis","s":"What is the direction of new DNA strand synthesis?","o":["5′ → 3′","3′ → 5′","Both directions on one strand","Random","Centre outwards only"],"e":"New molecules grow <b>5′ to 3′</b>: nucleotides added on the third carbon.","w":["Opposite.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Synthesis 5′→3′.","rn":[["","S stage","S stage","DNA synthesis"],["","What happens in the S stage?","What happens in the S stage?","DNA synthesis (replication)"]]},{"id":23072,"t":"Structure of DNA","s":"A template strand reads 3′–ATGC–5′. What is the new complementary strand (written 5′→3′)?","o":["5′–TACG–3′","5′–ATGC–3′","5′–GCAT–3′","5′–UACG–3′","5′–CGTA–3′"],"e":"Complementary pairing A–T, G–C; antiparallel: template 3′-ATGC-5′ pairs with 5′-TACG-3′.","w":["Same.","Not.","DNA.","Not."],"k":"Complementary antiparallel strand."},{"id":23073,"t":"Structure of DNA","s":"Which sugar is found in DNA, and how does it differ from RNA's sugar?","o":["Deoxyribose – H at 2′; ribose has OH at 2′","Ribose – OH at 2′; deoxyribose has H at 2′","Deoxyribose – OH at 2′; ribose has H at 2′","Glucose","Fructose"],"e":"Pentose sugar: <b>OH = ribose</b> (RNA), <b>H = deoxyribose</b> (DNA) at the 2′ position.","w":["RNA.","Swapped.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Deoxyribose: H at 2′.","rn":[["","Ribose vs deoxyribose: group at the 2' carbon","Ribose vs deoxyribose: group at the 2' carbon","Ribose: OH; Deoxyribose: H"]]},{"id":23074,"t":"Structure of DNA","s":"Which base is found in RNA but not DNA?","e":"Uracil replaces thymine in RNA.","k":"Uracil.","a":["uracil","u"],"rn":[["","Uracil replaces which DNA base in RNA?","Uracil replaces which DNA base in RNA?","Thymine"]]},{"id":23075,"t":"The Cell Cycle and Mitosis","s":"If a cell replicated its DNA but could not divide, why would that be harmful?","o":["It would make double the proteins","It would lose all of its DNA","It would enter G0 safely","It would have no chromosomes","It would become a prokaryote"],"e":"A cell cannot exist with double the DNA because it would make <b>double the proteins</b>.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Double DNA → double proteins.","rn":[["","Why must a cell die if it cannot repair after S phase?","Why must a cell die if it cannot repair after S phase?","It has double the DNA; Would make double the proteins"]]},{"id":23076,"t":"The Cell Cycle and Mitosis","s":"Why do normal cells divide?","o":["For growth or replacing damaged cells","Only cancer cells divide in adults; normal cells do not","Neurons divide constantly","Cells divide without any signal","Only in embryos"],"e":"Cells normally divide because we <b>grow</b> or cells have <b>died</b> (damaged epithelium, cut skin), usually after an <b>external signal</b>.","w":["Not.","Not.","Signal.","Not."],"k":"Normal division reasons.","rn":[["","What are 3 normal reasons cells need to divide?","What are 3 normal reasons cells need to divide?","Growth; Replacing dead/damaged cells (e.g. epithelium); Healing (e.g. cut skin)"]]},{"id":23077,"t":"The Cell Cycle and Mitosis","s":"Which pairing of mitotic structure and function is correct?","o":["Kinetochore – spindle attachment","Centromere – produces ATP for division","Nuclear envelope – pulls chromosomes","Astral microtubules – replicate DNA","Metaphase plate – re-forms nucleus"],"e":"The <b>kinetochore</b> (at the centromere) is where a chromosome joins the microtubules of the mitotic spindle.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Kinetochore attaches spindle.","rn":[["","Kinetochore","Kinetochore","Where a chromosome joins the microtubules (mitotic spindle)"],["","What is checked in mitosis?","What is checked in mitosis?","Chromosomes attached to the mitotic spindle"]]},{"id":23078,"t":"The Cell Cycle and Mitosis","s":"What makes up the mitotic spindle?","o":["Microtubules polymerising from centrosomes","Actin filaments polymerising from centrosomes","Histones","Keratin","Lamins"],"e":"Microtubules make the <b>mitotic spindle by polymerising</b> and guide separating chromosomes.","w":["Microtubules.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Spindle = microtubules.","rn":[["","What forms the mitotic spindle?","What forms the mitotic spindle?","Microtubules (polymerising)"]]},{"id":23079,"t":"DNA Replication","s":"Why are there always two copies of each chromosome before division?","o":["DNA was copied in S phase","Cells have two nuclei","Mitochondria contribute one copy","Histones copy chromosomes","Meiosis has occurred"],"e":"After S phase there are <b>two copies</b>; in mitosis one goes to each daughter cell.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Two copies → one each.","rn":[["","When does DNA replication happen?","When does DNA replication happen?","At certain times in the cell's life; S phase"],["","Why must a cell die if it cannot repair after S phase?","Why must a cell die if it cannot repair after S phase?","It has double the DNA; Would make double the proteins"]]},{"id":23080,"t":"The Human Genome","s":"What is the intergenic region?","o":["DNA between genes","Coding part of a gene","Non-coding part within a gene","RNA primer region","Histone binding site only"],"e":"The DNA <b>between genes</b> is the <b>intergenic region</b>.","w":["Exon.","Intron.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Intergenic = between genes.","rn":[["","Intergenic Region","Intergenic Region","DNA between genes; Sometimes associated with gene regulation"]]},{"id":23081,"t":"The Human Genome","s":"Which statement about the mitochondrial genome's non-coding DNA is correct?","o":["No repeats; 87 bp intergenic","Mostly repetitive DNA, with 87 bp intergenic","No repetitive DNA; 87 kbp intergenic","3.2 billion bp","Paternally inherited"],"e":"Mitochondrial genome: <b>no repetitive sequence</b>; only <b>87 bp intergenic</b> (in the <b>D-loop</b>).","w":["No repeats.","bp.","Nuclear.","Maternal."],"k":"Mito D-loop 87 bp.","rn":[["","How much of the mitochondrial genome is intergenic?","How much of the mitochondrial genome is intergenic?","Only 87 bp"]]},{"id":23082,"t":"The Human Genome","s":"Why is the gene number for humans described as approximate?","o":["It reflects genes identified so far","Genes change every day","It is a guess with no data","Only mitochondria were counted","It is exactly 20,000"],"e":"Gene number is still approximate – how many we have <b>identified</b>.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Approximate."],"k":"Gene count approximate."},{"id":23083,"t":"The Human Genome","s":"Which statement about the nuclear genome's composition is correct?","o":["Mostly repetitive elements","Mostly protein-coding exons","Entirely intergenic with no genes","A circular genome","Lacking introns"],"e":"Nuclear genome: <b>majority high copy number repetitive elements</b>.","w":["Not.","Not.","Linear.","Has."],"k":"Nuclear genome mostly repeats.","rn":[["","What makes up the majority of the nuclear genome?","What makes up the majority of the nuclear genome?","High copy number repetitive elements"]]},{"id":23085,"t":"DNA Packaging and Organisation","s":"Which statement about metaphase chromosomes is correct?","o":["Heterochromatin, ~10,000-fold","Euchromatin, ~1000-fold","Heterochromatin, ~1000-fold","Uncompacted","Without histones"],"e":"A chromosome in metaphase is heterochromatin – c.10,000-fold compaction.","w":["Not.","10,000.","Compacted.","Not."],"k":"Metaphase = heterochromatin.","rn":[["","Heterochromatin","Heterochromatin","Tightly packed chromatin; Compacted c.10,000 fold; Genes the cell does not need"],["","How much are metaphase chromosomes compacted?","How much are metaphase chromosomes compacted?","c.10,000 fold"]]},{"id":23086,"t":"DNA Packaging and Organisation","s":"What is the significance of DNA's small nucleus vs huge size?","o":["DNA must be packed, folded and compacted","The nucleus expands to fit uncompacted DNA","DNA lies mostly outside the nucleus","DNA is cut into pieces","DNA is discarded"],"e":"DNA is huge and the nucleus small, so DNA is <b>packed, folded and compacted</b>.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Compaction to fit.","rn":[["","Is DNA loose inside the nucleus?","Is DNA loose inside the nucleus?","No; Packed, folded and compacted"]]},{"id":23087,"t":"DNA Replication","s":"Name the replication-based lab technique routinely used in NHS pathology labs to amplify DNA.","e":"PCR uses DNA replication principles for diagnosis.","k":"PCR.","a":["pcr","polymerase chain reaction"],"rn":[["","Which lab technique came from describing DNA replication?","Which lab technique came from describing DNA replication?","Polymerase chain reaction (PCR)"]]},{"id":23088,"t":"DNA Replication","s":"Which mismatch recognition cue helps DNA polymerase detect errors?","o":["A wrong pair changes shape (a bulge)","A wrong pair changes colour","A wrong pair reverses charge","A wrong pair raises temperature","A wrong pair releases histones"],"e":"The wrong pairing changes the shape (one base two rings, one one ring; wrong combination makes a bulge), so polymerase recognises it.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Mismatch causes bulge.","rn":[["","How does polymerase recognise a wrong base?","How does polymerase recognise a wrong base?","Structure changes (a bulge)"]]},{"id":23089,"t":"The Cell Cycle and Mitosis","s":"Which events of the cell cycle occur in order after cytokinesis?","o":["G1 → S → G2 → M → cytokinesis","G1 → G2 → S → M → cytokinesis","S → G1 → G2 → M → cytokinesis","G1 → S → M → G2 → cytokinesis","M → S → G1 → G2"],"e":"Cycle: G1 (or G0) → S → G2 → M → cytokinesis → G1.","w":["S before G2.","G1 first.","G2 before M.","Not."],"k":"Cycle order.","rn":[["","What are the stages of the cell cycle in order?","What are the stages of the cell cycle in order?","G1; S; G2; M (mitosis); Cytokinesis"]]},{"id":23090,"t":"The Cell Cycle and Mitosis","s":"Most of our cells exist in which phase?","o":["G1","S","G2","M","Telophase"],"e":"<b>Most of our cells exist in G1</b>; some stay for months or years.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Most cells in G1.","rn":[["","How long can G1 last?","How long can G1 last?","Months to years; Depends on cell type"],["","What are the stages of the cell cycle in order?","What are the stages of the cell cycle in order?","G1; S; G2; M (mitosis); Cytokinesis"],["","Do all our cells have the same DNA?","Do all our cells have the same DNA?","Yes"]]},{"id":23091,"t":"The Cell Cycle and Mitosis","s":"What usually triggers a cell to leave G1 and divide?","o":["A signal, usually external","Random chance","DNA damage","Lack of nutrients","Nuclear envelope breakdown"],"e":"At some point a cell receives a <b>signal to divide</b>, most often external.","w":["Not.","Stops.","Stops.","Later."],"k":"External signal triggers division.","rn":[["","What starts a cell dividing?","What starts a cell dividing?","A signal; Most often external (environmental)"]]},{"id":23092,"t":"The Cell Cycle and Mitosis","s":"Which explains why chemotherapy is called “very crude”?","o":["Efficient, but hits all dividing cells","Efficient, but only hits G0 cells","It doesn’t work at all","It edits cancer genes","It only affects mitochondria"],"e":"Chemotherapy is “a very crude way” – very efficient, but targets <b>all dividing cells</b>.","w":["Dividing.","It works.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Chemo crude.","rn":[["","Why is chemotherapy efficient but crude?","Why is chemotherapy efficient but crude?","Very efficient; But crude (targets all dividing cells)"]]},{"id":23093,"t":"The Cell Cycle and Mitosis","s":"What happens to a cell whose checkpoint failure triggers death?","o":["Apoptosis and removal","It becomes a stem cell","It doubles its DNA again","It enters S phase","It becomes heterochromatin"],"e":"Cells that fail checkpoints (e.g. due to chemotherapy) stop dividing or die – <b>apoptosis</b> – and are removed.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Checkpoint failure → apoptosis.","rn":[["","What type of cell death do chemotherapy drugs trigger?","What type of cell death do chemotherapy drugs trigger?","Apoptosis"]]},{"id":23094,"t":"Structure of DNA","s":"What is a polymer, as defined?","o":["Repeated identical units","A single nucleotide","A sugar molecule","A hydrogen bond","A chromosome only"],"e":"A <b>polymer</b> is any molecule that is a <b>repetition of the same unit</b>; DNA is a polymer of nucleotides.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Polymer definition.","rn":[["","Polymer","Polymer","A molecule made of repeats of the same unit"]]},{"id":23095,"t":"Structure of DNA","s":"Which statement about the glycosidic bond formation is correct?","o":["A sugar; releases one water","It joins two phosphates","It is a hydrogen bond","It joins two bases","It requires ATP"],"e":"Glycosidic bond: involves a sugar, <b>releases one water molecule</b> on forming.","w":["Not.","Covalent.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Glycosidic releases water.","rn":[["","Glycosidic Bond","Glycosidic Bond","Joins the nitrogenous base to the sugar; A bond involving a sugar (carbohydrate); Releases one water molecule when it forms"]]},{"id":23099,"t":"The Human Genome","s":"A forensic lab matches crime scene DNA to a suspect. Which genomic feature is used?","o":["Individual SNP variation","Number of histones per cell","Mitochondrial genome size","Number of chromosomes","Okazaki fragments"],"e":"Forensics matches genetic material to a person via <b>SNP variations</b> – everybody has their own DNA identity.","w":["Same.","Same.","Same.","Not."],"k":"Forensics uses SNPs.","rn":[["","How are SNPs used in forensics?","How are SNPs used in forensics?","Match genetic material to a person; SNP variations unique to them"]]},{"id":23100,"t":"DNA Packaging and Organisation","s":"Which statement is true of all body cells regarding DNA content?","o":["Same DNA, but different genes used","Each cell type has unique DNA","Bone cells lack neurotransmitter genes","Only neurons have full DNA","Blood cells lack DNA"],"e":"<b>All our cells have the same DNA</b> but don't use or need the same proteins.","w":["Same.","Packed away.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Same DNA, different expression.","rn":[["","What are the 2 reasons for DNA packaging?","What are the 2 reasons for DNA packaging?","Saving space; Selective gene expression"],["","DNA is a polymer built from how many different nucleotides?","DNA is a polymer built from how many different nucleotides?","4"],["","What do the different domains of DNA polymerase do?","What do the different domains of DNA polymerase do?","Hold the DNA strands; Help add nucleotides"]]},{"id":23101,"t":"The Cell Cycle and Mitosis","s":"Which structure is labelled “centrosome (spindle pole)” in metaphase?","o":["The organising centre at each pole","The site where chromosomes attach to the microtubules","The plane where chromosomes align in metaphase","The re-forming nuclear envelope","A nucleosome"],"e":"Metaphase labels: centrosome (spindle pole), polar/kinetochore/astral microtubules, metaphase plate, nuclear vesicles.","w":["Kinetochore.","Plate.","Telophase.","Not."],"k":"Centrosome = spindle pole."},{"id":23102,"t":"The Cell Cycle and Mitosis","s":"Name the spindle microtubules that point out towards the cell edge in metaphase.","e":"Astral microtubules radiate to the cell edge; kinetochore microtubules attach to chromosomes.","k":"Astral microtubules.","a":["astral microtubules","astral","astral fibres"],"rn":[["","Kinetochore","Kinetochore","Where a chromosome joins the microtubules (mitotic spindle)"],["","In anaphase, what happens to kinetochore microtubules?","In anaphase, what happens to kinetochore microtubules?","They shorten"],["","What forms the mitotic spindle?","What forms the mitotic spindle?","Microtubules (polymerising)"]]},{"id":23103,"t":"DNA Replication","s":"About how many nucleotides long is an Okazaki fragment?","o":["Around 50 nucleotides","Millions of nucleotides","One nucleotide","Exactly 146 nucleotides","None"],"e":"On the lagging strand polymerase makes a small part (“some with like 50 nucleotides”), then stops.","w":["Not.","Not.","Nucleosome.","Not."],"k":"Small fragments.","rn":[["","About how long is one lagging strand fragment?","About how long is one lagging strand fragment?","About 50 nucleotides"]]},{"id":23104,"t":"DNA Packaging and Organisation","s":"Which statement about DNA packaging and gene expression is correct?","o":["Packing allows selective expression","Packing serves only to save space","All genes are equally accessible","Packing stops all transcription","Packing occurs only in bacteria"],"e":"Packing is for space-saving but also matters for <b>selective gene expression</b>.","w":["Not only.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Packaging regulates expression.","rn":[["","What are the 2 reasons for DNA packaging?","What are the 2 reasons for DNA packaging?","Saving space; Selective gene expression"]]},{"id":23105,"t":"The Human Genome","s":"One gene gives rise to several proteins by alternative splicing. What are these proteins called?","e":"Splicing combines exons in different ways, so ~20,000 genes give ~100,000 proteins.","k":"Isoforms.","a":["isoforms","isoform","protein isoforms","splice variants"],"rn":[["","Isoform","Isoform","One protein version of a gene; Some exons used only in one isoform"]]},{"id":23107,"t":"The Human Genome","s":"Which database gives detailed information on genetic diseases and their molecular basis?","o":["OMIM","UCSC genome browser","Rx Bricks","PubMed alone","Radiopaedia"],"e":"<b>OMIM</b> – a tremendous amount of detail about genetic disease.","w":["Gene structure.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"OMIM = genetic disease.","rn":[["","OMIM","OMIM","Online Mendelian Inheritance in Man"]]},{"id":23108,"t":"Structure of DNA","s":"A 10 bp stretch of DNA has 6 G–C and 4 A–T pairs. How many hydrogen bonds hold the strands together?","e":"6 × 3 + 4 × 2 = 18 + 8 = 26.","k":"26.","a":["26","twenty-six","twenty six"],"rn":[["","What bonds hold the two DNA strands together?","What bonds hold the two DNA strands together?","Hydrogen bonds between bases"]]},{"id":23109,"t":"The Cell Cycle and Mitosis","s":"A patient's tumour cells have lost a functioning G1 checkpoint. What might result?","o":["Damaged cells may enter S phase","Damaged cells will all enter G0 permanently","Damaged cells will be repaired perfectly","Cells will stop replicating altogether","No effect on the cell cycle"],"e":"The cycle is very tightly regulated; if not, division can happen uncontrollably → malignancy (“often, not always”).","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Effect."],"k":"Lost checkpoint → uncontrolled division.","rn":[["","How do cell cycle checkpoints prevent disease?","How do cell cycle checkpoints prevent disease?","Stop damaged or incompletely replicated DNA dividing; Prevent uncontrolled division (malignancy)"]]},{"id":23111,"t":"Structure of DNA","s":"Which pairing of measurement and DNA helix feature is correct?","o":["0.34 nm – between base pairs","3.4 nm – helix diameter","2 nm – one full turn","146 bp – one turn","10 nm – base spacing"],"e":"3.4 nm = one full turn; 0.34 nm = between base pairs; 2 nm = diameter.","w":["Turn.","Diameter.","Nucleosome.","Not."],"k":"0.34 nm between bases.","rn":[["","What is the distance between adjacent base pairs?","What is the distance between adjacent base pairs?","0.34 nm"]]},{"id":23112,"t":"DNA Packaging and Organisation","s":"Which proteins form the octameric nucleosome core?","o":["H2A, H2B, H3, H4 (two of each)","H1, H2A, H2B, H3 (two of each)","H1 only (eight copies)","Cyclins","Actin and myosin"],"e":"Octameric core: <b>2 copies each of H2A, H2B, H3 and H4</b>.","w":["No H1.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Histone octamer.","rn":[["","What are the 4 histones of the octameric core?","What are the 4 histones of the octameric core?","H2A; H2B; H3; H4"],["","Image card: What is this structure? What is its core made of?","What is this structure? What is its core made of?","Nucleosome; Octamer: 2 copies H2A, H2B, H3, H4; 146 bp DNA wrapped around"]]},{"id":23113,"t":"Structure of DNA","s":"Which statement links DNA structure and replication?","o":["DNA structure influences replication","DNA structure is irrelevant to replication","Replication determines histone number","Replication occurs only in mitochondria","Structure prevents replication"],"e":"Take-home: the genome defines our biology; variation makes genomics critical; <b>structure of DNA influences replication</b>; cell cycle/mitosis control is critical to health.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Structure influences replication.","rn":[["","Who else defined the structure of DNA?","Who else defined the structure of DNA?","Watson and Crick"],["","Replication Fork","Replication Fork","Where the DNA opens and polymerase makes new DNA"]]},{"id":23115,"t":"The Human Genome","s":"Which RNA molecules are encoded by the mitochondrial genome?","o":["22 tRNAs and 2 rRNAs","2 tRNAs and 22 rRNAs","Only mRNA","No RNA","100 tRNAs"],"e":"Mitochondrial genes: <b>22 tRNA</b>, <b>2 rRNA</b>, <b>13 protein-coding</b>.","w":["Swapped.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Mito: 22 tRNA, 2 rRNA, 13 protein.","rn":[["","What genes are in the mitochondrial genome?","What genes are in the mitochondrial genome?","22 tRNA; 2 rRNA; 13 protein coding"]]},{"id":23116,"t":"DNA Replication","s":"Which statement about DNA replication timing is correct?","o":["In S phase before division","In mitosis","Continuously throughout life","In G0","After cytokinesis only"],"e":"Replication happens at certain times – the <b>S stage</b>, before division.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Replication in S phase.","rn":[["","When does DNA replication happen?","When does DNA replication happen?","At certain times in the cell's life; S phase"]]},{"id":23117,"t":"DNA Replication","s":"Which correctly describes base pairing complementarity?","o":["A–T and G–C","A–G and C–T","A–C and G–T","U–G in DNA","Any base with any"],"e":"A–T and G–C.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"A–T, G–C.","rn":[["","Which lab technique came from describing DNA replication?","Which lab technique came from describing DNA replication?","Polymerase chain reaction (PCR)"],["","bp","bp","Base pairs"]]},{"id":23118,"t":"DNA Packaging and Organisation","s":"How much DNA is wrapped around each nucleosome histone core?","o":["146 bp","1,330 bp","16.6 kbp","87 bp","50 nucleotides"],"e":"The fundamental unit of chromatin is the nucleosome: an octamer of histones (2 copies each of H2A, H2B, H3, H4) wrapped with 146 bp of DNA.","w":["Average spacing of SNPs.","The mitochondrial genome.","The mitochondrial intergenic region.","About the size of an Okazaki fragment."],"k":"Lambert 2013 GWAS.","rn":[["","Image card: What is this structure? What is its core made of?","What is this structure? What is its core made of?","Nucleosome; Octamer: 2 copies H2A, H2B, H3, H4; 146 bp DNA wrapped around"],["","Nucleosome","Nucleosome","8 histones (octamer); Wrapped with 146 bp of DNA"]]},{"id":23119,"t":"DNA Replication","s":"Which requirements does the lagging strand have that the leading strand does not?","o":["Multiple primers and joining of fragments by ligase","A single primer and joining of fragments by helicase","No primers, but joining by ligase","Multiple primers, but no joining step","A separate DNA polymerase that works 3′ → 5′"],"e":"The leading strand needs one primer and is made continuously. The lagging strand needs multiple primers (one per Okazaki fragment); the RNA primers are later replaced by DNA and the fragments joined by ligase.","w":["Ligase, multiple primers.","Primers needed.","Fragments must be joined.","Polymerase only adds to 3′ ends."],"k":"King 2019.","rn":[["","What joins the Okazaki fragments together?","What joins the Okazaki fragments together?","Ligase"],["","Leading vs lagging strand: number of primers","Leading vs lagging strand: number of primers","Leading: one primer; Lagging: multiple primers"]]},{"id":23120,"t":"The Cell Cycle and Mitosis","s":"Using P-M-A-T, which stage of mitosis directly follows metaphase?","o":["Anaphase","Telophase","Prophase","Cytokinesis","G2"],"e":"Mitosis stages in order: prophase, metaphase, anaphase, telophase (P-M-A-T). In metaphase chromosomes align on the metaphase plate; in telophase separation is complete, the nuclear envelope re-forms and chromosomes decondense.","w":["Telophase follows anaphase.","Prophase precedes metaphase.","After telophase.","Before mitosis."],"k":"Lecturer contact.","rn":[["","What are the 4 events of mitosis in order?","What are the 4 events of mitosis in order?","Prophase; Metaphase; Anaphase; Telophase; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":23121,"t":"The Cell Cycle and Mitosis","s":"A 50-year-old woman starts chemotherapy for cancer. The drugs target cells in the phase where DNA is synthesised and the phase where chromosomes are separated. After a few weeks she loses her hair and becomes anaemic, even though her hair follicles and bone marrow are not cancerous.<br><br>Which phases does the chemotherapy target, and why do these side effects occur?","o":["S and M phases; all fast-dividing cells, such as hair and bone marrow, are affected","G1 and G0; resting cells are killed","S and M phases; only cancer cells divide, so the side effects are unrelated","G2 only; it blocks the G2 checkpoint in cancer cells","M phase only; hair and marrow cells are in G0 and are damaged by a separate route"],"e":"Step 1: the cell cycle runs G1 (with G0 for non-dividing cells), S (DNA synthesis), G2, then mitosis (M) and cytokinesis, and chemotherapy targets the S and M phases. Step 2: because it targets dividing cells, it hits every fast-dividing cell, not just cancer cells, causing hair loss (hair follicles) and anaemia (bone marrow).","w":["Chemotherapy targets dividing S and M phase cells, not resting cells.","Other fast-dividing cells are affected too.","The lecture says S and M phases are targeted.","S phase is also targeted, and hair and marrow cells divide quickly."],"k":"Chemotherapy targets S and M → hits all fast-dividing cells.","rn":[["","What are 2 side effects of chemotherapy?","What are 2 side effects of chemotherapy?","Anaemia (bone marrow); Hair loss"],["","Why does chemotherapy cause anaemia?","Why does chemotherapy cause anaemia?","Bone marrow has dividing cells; Constantly makes blood cells"],["","Why do chemotherapy drugs target malignant cells?","Why do chemotherapy drugs target malignant cells?","They are fast dividing"]]},{"id":23122,"t":"The Cell Cycle and Mitosis","s":"A cell is exposed to radiation just before it is due to copy its DNA, and its DNA is damaged. A second cell finishes copying its DNA but some regions are incompletely replicated. A third cell reaches mitosis but some chromosomes are not attached to the spindle.<br><br>Which checkpoint stops each cell, and what controls the checkpoints?","o":["1: G1; 2: G2; 3: M; controlled by cyclins and protein kinases","1: M; 2: G1; 3: G2; controlled by DNA ligase","1: G2; 2: M; 3: G1; controlled by histones","All stopped at G0 by DNA polymerase","1: G1; 2: S; 3: G2; controlled by the exonuclease activity of DNA polymerase, which proofreads every strand"],"e":"Step 1: the G1 checkpoint (before S) stops the cycle if DNA is damaged or the environment is unfavourable; the end of S/G2 checkpoint stops it if DNA is damaged or incompletely replicated; the M checkpoint stops it if chromosomes are not attached to the mitotic spindle. Step 2: checkpoints are controlled by cyclins and protein kinases.","w":["The checkpoints are mismatched, and ligase joins DNA fragments.","The checkpoints are mismatched, and histones package DNA.","G0 is a resting state, not a checkpoint.","Unattached chromosomes are checked at M, and cyclins/kinases control checkpoints."],"k":"G1: damage/environment; G2: damage/incomplete; M: spindle attachment; cyclins + kinases.","rn":[["","Checkpoints are controlled by what?","Checkpoints are controlled by what?","Cyclins; Protein kinases"]]},{"id":23123,"t":"DNA Replication","s":"A student models DNA replication at a fork. On one template strand, a single RNA primer allows continuous synthesis toward the fork as it opens. On the other strand, synthesis happens in short pieces, each needing a new primer, which are later joined.<br><br>Why do the two strands differ, and what joins the short pieces?","o":["DNA is antiparallel and synthesis only adds to the 3′ end; ligase joins the fragments","One strand lacks a 3′ end, so it needs no primer; helicase joins the fragments","Both strands are made 3′ to 5′; DNA polymerase joins the fragments","The lagging strand is made by RNA polymerase; histones join the fragments","Replication is conservative, so one strand is copied whole and the other in pieces held together by the nucleosome"],"e":"Step 1: DNA is antiparallel, and nucleotides can only be added to the 3′ –OH end (5′ to 3′ synthesis), so one strand (leading) is made continuously while the other (lagging) is made discontinuously, needing further primer binding. Step 2: DNA polymerase needs an RNA primer with a free 3′ end, and ligase joins the fragments (Okazaki fragments) of the lagging strand; overall replication is semi-conservative.","w":["Both strands need primers, and ligase joins fragments.","Synthesis is 5′ to 3′, adding to the 3′ end.","DNA polymerase makes both strands; histones package DNA.","Replication is semi-conservative."],"k":"Antiparallel + 3′ addition → leading (continuous), lagging (fragments joined by ligase).","rn":[["","What joins the Okazaki fragments together?","What joins the Okazaki fragments together?","Ligase"],["","Leading vs lagging strand: synthesis","Leading vs lagging strand: synthesis","Leading: continuous; Lagging: discontinuous (fragments)"]]},{"id":23124,"t":"DNA Replication","s":"During replication, DNA polymerase adds a wrong base to the end of a growing strand. The enzyme detects the mismatch, removes that final nucleotide and then adds the correct one. A different enzyme in the cell can cut through the middle of a DNA chain.<br><br>Which activity does DNA polymerase use for proofreading, and what is the other activity called?","o":["Exonuclease (removes the end nucleotide); endonuclease (cuts through the chain)","Endonuclease (removes the end nucleotide); exonuclease (cuts through the chain)","Ligase activity; helicase activity","Histone acetyl-transferase activity; kinase activity","Primase activity, which lays down a new RNA primer; then ligase activity to seal the new base in place"],"e":"Step 1: exonuclease activity removes a final (end) nucleotide, and this is the basis of DNA polymerase's proofreading. Step 2: endonuclease activity cuts through a DNA chain rather than removing an end nucleotide.","w":["The two activities are swapped.","Ligase joins fragments; it is not the proofreading activity.","These are not proofreading activities.","Proofreading removes the wrong nucleotide by exonuclease activity."],"k":"DNA polymerase proofreads with exonuclease activity (removes end nucleotide).","rn":[["","Exonuclease Activity","Exonuclease Activity","Removing the final nucleotide of a chain"]]},{"id":23125,"t":"Structure of DNA","s":"A researcher heats two DNA samples of equal length. Sample X is rich in guanine and cytosine, and sample Y is rich in adenine and thymine. Sample Y separates into single strands at a lower temperature than sample X.<br><br>Which explanation is correct?","o":["G–C pairs have three hydrogen bonds, A–T only two, so G–C-rich DNA is harder to separate","A–T pairs have three hydrogen bonds, so Y is more stable","G–C pairs are joined by phosphodiester bonds","Y is single-stranded already because A–T cannot pair","X is more stable because purines pair with purines in G–C pairs, which are larger than A–T pairs"],"e":"Step 1: in the double helix, complementary bases pair across the strands: A with T via two hydrogen bonds and G with C via three. Step 2: G–C-rich DNA therefore has more hydrogen bonds holding the strands together and needs more energy (higher temperature) to separate.","w":["A–T pairs have two hydrogen bonds.","Phosphodiester bonds join sugars along a strand, not bases across strands.","A and T do pair, with two hydrogen bonds.","Purines pair with pyrimidines (G with C), and the difference is hydrogen bonds."],"k":"A–T: 2 H-bonds; G–C: 3 H-bonds → G–C-rich DNA more stable.","rn":[["","What bonds hold the two DNA strands together?","What bonds hold the two DNA strands together?","Hydrogen bonds between bases"],["","Image card: How many hydrogen bonds join this base pair?","How many hydrogen bonds join this base pair?","2 (A and T)"],["","Image card: How many hydrogen bonds join this base pair?","How many hydrogen bonds join this base pair?","3 (C and G)"]]},{"id":23126,"t":"DNA Packaging and Organisation","s":"In a liver cell, genes for liver enzymes are transcribed constantly, while genes needed only in nerve cells are never used. A researcher finds that the liver enzyme genes lie in loosely packed chromatin, while the nerve genes lie in tightly packed chromatin, about ten times more compacted.<br><br>What are these two forms of chromatin?","o":["Liver genes in euchromatin (~1000×); nerve genes in heterochromatin (~10,000×)","Liver genes in heterochromatin; nerve genes in euchromatin","Both in euchromatin, which is the only form in interphase","Liver genes in mitochondrial DNA; nerve genes in the nucleus","Liver genes on the metaphase chromosome; nerve genes on the nucleosome core of eight histones"],"e":"Step 1: euchromatin is compacted about 1000-fold and loosely packed, so genes the cell needs are more accessible and transcribed regularly. Step 2: heterochromatin (like metaphase chromosomes) is compacted about 10,000-fold and tightly packed, holding genes the cell does not need.","w":["The forms are swapped.","Interphase cells contain both forms.","Liver enzyme genes are nuclear.","Metaphase chromosomes are highly compacted; nucleosomes are present in all chromatin."],"k":"Euchromatin (~1000×, active); heterochromatin (~10,000×, silent).","rn":[["","Heterochromatin","Heterochromatin","Tightly packed chromatin; Compacted c.10,000 fold; Genes the cell does not need"],["","Euchromatin","Euchromatin","Loosely packed chromatin; Compacted c.1000 fold; Genes the cell needs"]]},{"id":23127,"t":"The Human Genome","s":"A family has a disorder caused by a mutation in a gene found in a small circular genome of 16.6 kbp that codes for 13 proteins, 22 tRNAs and 2 rRNAs. Affected mothers pass the disorder to all their children, but affected fathers do not pass it on.<br><br>Which genome carries the mutation, and why does this pattern occur?","o":["The mitochondrial genome; it is maternally inherited","The nuclear genome; it has 3.2 billion base pairs","The X chromosome; it is passed only by mothers","The mitochondrial genome; it is inherited equally from both parents","The nuclear genome; it contains 20,000 genes and is passed only from the father to his children"],"e":"Step 1: the mitochondrial genome is 16.6 kbp with 22 tRNA genes, 2 rRNA genes and 13 protein-coding genes, with almost no intergenic sequence. Step 2: it shows maternal inheritance, so affected mothers pass it to all children while fathers do not.","w":["The nuclear genome is far larger, and the pattern is maternal.","The genome described is mitochondrial, not the X chromosome.","Mitochondrial inheritance is maternal.","The genome described is the small mitochondrial one."],"k":"Mitochondrial genome: 16.6 kbp, 37 genes, maternal inheritance.","rn":[["","What genes are in the mitochondrial genome?","What genes are in the mitochondrial genome?","22 tRNA; 2 rRNA; 13 protein coding"],["","How is mitochondrial DNA inherited?","How is mitochondrial DNA inherited?","Maternal inheritance"],["","How big is the mitochondrial genome?","How big is the mitochondrial genome?","16.6 thousand base pairs (kbp)"]]},{"id":23128,"t":"The Human Genome","s":"Humans have about 20,000 genes but produce about 100,000 different proteins. A researcher studies one gene and finds that two of its exons are used in one version of the protein but not in another, made in a different tissue.<br><br>Which process explains the larger number of proteins?","o":["Splicing of exons produces different isoforms, giving a larger proteome","Introns are translated into extra proteins","Each gene is copied 5 times in the genome","Mitochondrial DNA codes for the extra 80,000 proteins","SNPs every 1,330 bp each create a brand new gene that codes for a different protein"],"e":"Step 1: genes contain exons and non-coding introns, separated by intergenic regions. Step 2: splicing can include or leave out exons (e.g. two exons used in only one isoform), so one gene can make several proteins, resulting in a larger proteome than the genome.","w":["Introns are non-coding.","Genes are not described as being copied 5 times.","The mitochondrial genome codes only 13 proteins.","SNPs are variants, not new genes."],"k":"Splicing exons → isoforms → proteome > genome.","rn":[["","What does splicing result in?","What does splicing result in?","A larger proteome (compared to the genome)"],["","Isoform","Isoform","One protein version of a gene; Some exons used only in one isoform"]]},{"id":23129,"t":"The Human Genome","s":"Two research projects study disease genetics. Project A finds a rare variant in a single family that, on its own, causes a disease with a clear Mendelian pattern. Project B scans the genomes of thousands of people and finds many common variants that each slightly raise the risk of a disease.<br><br>Which statement best describes the two types of variant?","o":["A: rare, high effect size; B: common, low effect size found by GWAS","A: common, high effect; B: rare, low effect","A: common, low effect found by GWAS; B: rare, Mendelian","Both are rare variants of large effect","A: a structural variant on the mitochondrial genome; B: a SNP in a nucleosome, which has no effect on disease risk"],"e":"Step 1: rare alleles (low frequency) with high effect size cause Mendelian disease, as in project A. Step 2: common variants (high frequency) with low effect size are identified by genome wide association studies (GWAS), as in project B; SNPs are the most common genetic variant.","w":["Mendelian variants are rare, and GWAS finds common variants.","The two descriptions are swapped.","Project B finds common variants of small effect.","The variants differ in frequency and effect; they are not described as mitochondrial."],"k":"Rare + high effect = Mendelian; common + low effect = GWAS.","rn":[["","What are the 4 variant groups by frequency and effect?","What are the 4 variant groups by frequency and effect?","Rare alleles causing Mendelian disease (high effect); Rare variants of small effect; Common variants identified by GWAS (low effect); Common variants with a large effect"]]},{"id":23130,"t":"The Human Genome","s":"A child has intellectual disability, facial differences and broad thumbs and great toes. Genetic testing shows a mutation in CREBBP, a histone acetyl-transferase. A second, unrelated boy has X-linked intellectual disability, skeletal malformation and growth retardation from a mutation in RPS6KA3, a gene that regulates CREBBP.<br><br>Which syndromes are these, and what links them?","o":["Rubinstein-Taybi and Coffin-Lowry; both involve faults in DNA packaging via CREBBP","Coffin-Lowry and Rubinstein-Taybi; both are mitochondrial","Rubinstein-Taybi and Coffin-Lowry; both are caused by faulty DNA polymerase","Both are Coffin-Lowry; both are X-linked","Rubinstein-Taybi and Coffin-Lowry; both are caused by common low-effect variants found by GWAS"],"e":"Step 1: Rubinstein-Taybi syndrome (non-specific symptoms, facial abnormalities, broad thumbs and great toes) is caused by mutation in the histone acetyl-transferase CREBBP, while Coffin-Lowry syndrome (X-linked intellectual disability, skeletal malformation, growth retardation) is caused by mutation in RPS6KA3, which regulates CREBBP. Step 2: CREBBP modifies histones, so both diseases come from faults in DNA packaging.","w":["The names are swapped, and neither is mitochondrial.","Neither is caused by faulty DNA polymerase.","Only the second is Coffin-Lowry.","Both are rare high-effect mutations."],"k":"Rubinstein-Taybi (CREBBP) and Coffin-Lowry (RPS6KA3): DNA packaging faults.","rn":[["","Rubinstein-Taybi vs Coffin-Lowry: gene","Rubinstein-Taybi vs Coffin-Lowry: gene","Rubinstein-Taybi: CREBBP; Coffin-Lowry: RPS6KA3"]]},{"id":23521,"t":"DNA Packaging and Organisation","s":"How many histone proteins make up the core labelled <b>A</b>? Give a number.","a":["8","eight"],"e":"A is the histone octamer: 2 copies each of H2A, H2B, H3 and H4.","k":"Octamer = 8.","img":{"src":"23/o_chromatin","cap":"DNA packaging. Image: David O Morgan, Attribution, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","Image card: What is this structure? What is its core made of?","What is this structure? What is its core made of?","Nucleosome; Octamer: 2 copies H2A, H2B, H3, H4; 146 bp DNA wrapped around"]]},{"id":23522,"t":"DNA Packaging and Organisation","s":"Name unit <b>C</b>, the fundamental unit of chromatin, giving the “beads-on-a-string” look.","a":["nucleosome","nucleosomes"],"e":"C is the nucleosome: 8 histones wrapped with about 146 bp of DNA. Nucleosomes form beads-on-a-string and are coiled into fibres (D) and loops.","k":"C = nucleosome.","img":{"src":"23/o_chromatin","cap":"DNA packaging. Image: David O Morgan, Attribution, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","Nucleosome","Nucleosome","8 histones (octamer); Wrapped with 146 bp of DNA"]]},{"id":23523,"t":"DNA Packaging and Organisation","s":"About how much DNA (<b>B</b>) wraps around each unit C?","o":["About 146 bp","About 14 bp","About 1,460 bp","About 46 bp","About 10,000 bp"],"w":["Far too short to wrap a histone core.","Ten times too long.","Too short.","10,000 is the overall compaction fold, not bp."],"e":"About 146 bp per nucleosome (the image source gives 147). DNA is compacted about 10,000-fold in metaphase chromosomes.","k":"146 bp per nucleosome.","img":{"src":"23/o_chromatin","cap":"DNA packaging. Image: David O Morgan, Attribution, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","Nucleosome","Nucleosome","8 histones (octamer); Wrapped with 146 bp of DNA"],["","Image card: What is this structure? What is its core made of?","What is this structure? What is its core made of?","Nucleosome; Octamer: 2 copies H2A, H2B, H3, H4; 146 bp DNA wrapped around"]]},{"id":23524,"t":"DNA Packaging and Organisation","s":"Further coiling of D into fibres and loops compacts metaphase chromosome DNA by roughly how much?","o":["About 10,000-fold","About 10-fold","About 100-fold","About 146-fold","About 1,000,000-fold"],"w":["Far too little.","Too little.","146 is bp per nucleosome.","Too much."],"e":"Metaphase chromosome DNA is compacted about 10,000-fold: nucleosomes (C) → fibres (D) → loops.","k":"~10,000-fold.","img":{"src":"23/o_chromatin","cap":"DNA packaging. Image: David O Morgan, Attribution, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","How much are metaphase chromosomes compacted?","How much are metaphase chromosomes compacted?","c.10,000 fold"]]}]);
